/-!
# Detecting zero objects of `D(𝒪_{ℙⁿ_R})` by maps out of the twists `𝒪, …, 𝒪(-n)`

Let `R` be a ring and `P = ℙⁿ_R = Proj R[T₀, …, Tₙ]`. An object `Z` of the derived category of
`𝒪_P`-modules which is bounded below and has quasi-coherent cohomology sheaves, and which receives
only zero maps from all shifts of `𝒪, 𝒪(-1), …, 𝒪(-n)` (placed in degree zero), is zero.

The argument has two halves.

* **Generation.** By the Koszul step of [Stacks, Tag 0A9V]
  (`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ExampleGenerator.twist_neg_mem_gen`) every twist `𝒪(-m)`, `m ≥ 0`,
  lies in the subcategory `⟨E⟩` generated by `E = 𝒪 ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ 𝒪(-n)`, and by [Stacks, Tag 09SK] the
  vanishing of `Hom(E, Z⟦i⟧)` for all `i` propagates to every object of `⟨E⟩`. So all maps
  `𝒪(-m)⟦j⟧ ⟶ Z` vanish.
* **Detection.** If `Z ≠ 0`, let `k` be the lowest degree with `H^k(Z) ≠ 0`. Then `Z` is `≥ k`, so
  the truncation `τ^{≤k} Z ≅ H^k(Z)[-k]` maps to `Z` by an isomorphism on `H^k`, and a nonzero map
  `𝒪(-m) ⟶ H^k(Z)` gives a map `𝒪(-m)[-k] ⟶ Z` which is nonzero on `H^k`. Such a nonzero map
  exists because `H^k(Z)` is a nonzero quasi-coherent module and `𝒪(1)` is ample: by the module
  form of [Stacks, Tag 0BQR] some `H^k(Z) ⊗ 𝒪(m)` has a nonzero global section.

The derived category is the locally small model of
`StacksFrontier/Homology/DerivedCategory/LocallySmall.lean` (the global instance
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.hasDerivedCategory`), the one at which the twisted inverse image
of `ℙⁿ_R ⟶ Spec R` exists, and the one at which the generation step is proved.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Duality.ProjCoeff.isZero_of_hom_twist_eq_zero`: the detection statement above.
* `Stacks.Duality.ProjCoeff.hom_twist_neg_eq_zero`: maps out of all shifted twists `𝒪(-m)`,
  `m ≥ 0`, vanish once those out of `𝒪, …, 𝒪(-n)` do.
* `Stacks.Duality.ProjCoeff.exists_hom_twist_ne_zero`: a nonzero quasi-coherent module on `ℙⁿ_R`
  receives a nonzero map from some `𝒪(-m)`.
* `Stacks.Duality.ProjCoeff.exists_hom_singleFunctor_ne_zero_of_isGE`: for `Z ≥ k`, a nonzero map
  into `H^k(Z)` lifts to a nonzero map from the corresponding single complex in degree `k`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A9V](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A9V)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09SK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09SK)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BQR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BQR)
-/

